[[The Kiwi Party]], the [[New Citizen Party]] and the [[Jim Anderton's Progressive Party|Progressive Party]] were registered, but did not contend the election under their own banners. The Kiwi Party and the New Citizen Party stood candidates for the Conservative Party.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/5807380/New-Citizens-Party-joins-with-Conservatives |title=New Citizens Party joins with Conservatives |author=Danya Levy |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=18 October 2011 |access-date=18 October 2011 |archive-date=20 October 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111020051953/http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/5807380/New-Citizens-Party-joins-with-Conservatives |url-status=live }}</ref>

In addition to the registered parties and their candidates, thirteen candidates from nine non-registered parties contested electorates. The [[Human Rights Party (New Zealand)|Human Rights Party]] contested Auckland Central, the [[Communist League (New Zealand)|Communist League]] Manukau East and Mount Roskill, the [[Nga Iwi Morehu Movement]] contested Hauraki-Waikato and Te Tai Hauauru, the [[Pirate Party of New Zealand|Pirate Party]] contested Hamilton East and Wellington Central, the [[New Zealand Sovereignty Party|Sovereignty Party]] contested Clutha-Southland and Te Tai Hauauru, Economic Euthenics contested Wigram, [[New Economics Party|New Economics]] contested Wellington Central, [[Restore All Things In Christ]] contested Dunedin South, and the Youth Party contested West Coast-Tasman.

Seventeen [[Independent politician|independent]] candidates also contested the electorates in thirteen electorates: Christchurch Central, Coromandel, Epsom (two), Hamilton West (two), New Plymouth, Ōtaki, Rangitikei (two), Rongotai, Tāmaki (two), Tauranga, Waitaki, Wellington Central, and Ikaroa-Rawhiti

==Campaigning==
{{expand section|date=October 2011}}

===Epsom and the Tea Tape scandal===
{{main|Tea Tape scandal}}
On 11 November, National Party leader John Key met with [[John Banks (New Zealand politician)|John Banks]], the ACT candidate for [[Epsom (New Zealand electorate)|Epsom]], over a cup of tea at a cafe in [[Newmarket, New Zealand|Newmarket]] to send a signal to Epsom voters about voting tactically.<ref>{{cite web |last= McMahon |first= Lyle |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/5948536/PM-and-Banks-have-their-Epsom-cup-of-tea |title= PM and Banks have their Epsom cup of tea |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date= 11 November 2011 |access-date= 18 November 2011 |archive-date= 13 November 2011 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20111113162056/http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/5948536/PM-and-Banks-have-their-Epsom-cup-of-tea |url-status= live }}</ref> The National Party passively campaigned for Epsom voters to give their electorate vote to ACT while giving their party vote to National. This would allow ACT to bypass the 5% party vote threshold and enter Parliament by winning an electorate seat, thereby providing a coalition partner for National. However, in October and November 2011, [[Opinion polling for the 2011 New Zealand general election#Epsom electorate vote|polls of the Epsom electorate vote]] taken by various companies showed that the National candidate for Epsom, Paul Goldsmith,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/epsom-street-survey-goldsmith-on-top/O2YOKNFGKB55I4X3Z2NP2RTXMA/ |title=Epsom street survey: Goldsmith on top |publisher=[[New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 November 2011 |access-date=25 September 2021 |archive-date=25 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210925104510/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/epsom-street-survey-goldsmith-on-top/O2YOKNFGKB55I4X3Z2NP2RTXMA/ |url-status=live }}</ref> was leading in the polls and likely to win the seat. During the meeting, the two politicians' discussion was recorded by a device left on the table in a black pouch. The recording tapes were leaked to ''[[The Herald on Sunday]]'' newspaper, and subsequently created a media frenzy over the content of the unreleased tapes.<ref name="3 News">{{cite news |url=http://www.3news.co.nz/Key-may-face-more-teapot-tape-accusations/tabid/419/articleID/232874/Default.aspx |title=Key may face more teapot tape accusations |author=Wilson, Peter |date=16 November 2011 |work=[[3 News]] |location=New Zealand |access-date=16 November 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120201110834/http://www.3news.co.nz/Key-may-face-more-teapot-tape-accusations/tabid/419/articleID/232874/Default.aspx |archive-date=1 February 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

