===Electorate results===
[[File:New Zealand electorates 2011 election (insets).svg|thumb|left|600px|Party affiliation of winning electorate candidates.]]
{{clear}}
Prior to the election, the National Party held the majority of the electorate seats with 41. Labour held 20 seats, Māori held four seats, and ACT, Mana, Progressive, United Future and an ex-Labour independent held one seat each.

After the election, National gained one seat to hold 42 seats, Labour gained three seats to hold 23 electorates, Māori lost one seat to hold three, and ACT, Mana, and United Future held steady with one seat each.<ref name="electorate-status">{{cite web |url= https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2011/electoratestatus.html |title= Official Count Results – Electorate Status |publisher= Electoral Commission |date= 10 December 2011 |access-date= 25 September 2021 |archive-date= 7 June 2020 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20200607215222/https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2011/electoratestatus.html |url-status= live }}</ref> A National or Labour candidate took second place in all the general electorates except [[Rodney (New Zealand electorate)|Rodney]], where it was Conservative Party leader [[Colin Craig]].<ref>{{cite news |title=10 things you didn't know about the election results |first=Audrey |last=Young |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/election-2011/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503012&objectid=10769537 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=29 November 2011 |access-date=29 November 2011 |archive-date=29 November 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111129023815/http://www.nzherald.co.nz/election-2011/news/article.cfm?c_id=1503012&objectid=10769537 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In eleven electorates, the incumbents did not seek re-election, and new MPs were elected. In [[Coromandel (New Zealand electorate)|Coromandel]], [[North Shore (New Zealand electorate)|North Shore]], [[Northland (New Zealand electorate)|Northland]], [[Rangitīkei (New Zealand electorate)|Rangitikei]], Rodney and [[Tāmaki (New Zealand electorate)|Tāmaki]], the seats were passed from incumbent National MPs to new National MPs; in [[Epsom (New Zealand electorate)|Epsom]], the seat was passed from the incumbent ACT MP to the new ACT MP; and in [[Dunedin North (New Zealand electorate)|Dunedin North]] and [[Manurewa (New Zealand electorate)|Manurewa]], the seats were passed from incumbent Labour MPs to new Labour MPs. Labour also won [[Te Atatū (New Zealand electorate)|Te Atatū]] from the retiring ex-Labour independent, and [[Wigram (New Zealand electorate)|Wigram]] from the retiring Progressive MP.

Of the 59 seats where the incumbent sought re-election, four changed hands. In [[West Coast-Tasman]], Labour's Damien O'Connor regained the seat from National's Chris Auchinvole, who defeated him for the seat in 2008. In [[Waimakariri (New Zealand electorate)|Waimakariri]], National's Kate Wilkinson defeated Labour MP Clayton Cosgrove, and in [[Te Tai Tonga]], Labour's Rino Tirikatene defeated Maori Party MP Rahui Katene. [[Christchurch Central]] on election night ended with incumbent Labour MP [[Brendon Burns (politician)|Brendon Burns]] and National's [[Nicky Wagner]] [[tie (draw)|tied]] on 10,493 votes each, and on official counts, swung to Nicky Wagner with a 45-vote majority, increasing to 47 votes on a [[judicial recount]].<ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/study/news/christchurch-central-judicial-recount-official-results.html |title= Christchurch Central Judicial Recount Official Results |publisher=Electoral Commission |date= 14 December 2011 |access-date= 16 December 2011 |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120209195846/http://www.elections.org.nz/study/news/christchurch-central-judicial-recount-official-results.html |archive-date= 9 February 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/6137499/Recount-confirms-Christchurch-central-seat |access-date= 14 December 2011 |title= Recount confirms Christchurch Central seat |first= Kate |last= Chapman |date= 14 December 2011 |publisher= Fairfax Media (via Stuff.co.nz) |archive-date= 30 December 2013 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20131230232938/http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/6137499/Recount-confirms-Christchurch-central-seat |url-status= live }}</ref> Despite losing their electorate seats, Chris Auchinvole and Clayton Cosgrove were re-elected into parliament via the party list.

On election night, [[Waitakere (New Zealand electorate)|Waitakere]] was won by incumbent National MP [[Paula Bennett]] with a 349-vote majority over Labour's [[Carmel Sepuloni]]. On official counts, it swung to Sepuloni with a majority of 11 votes, and Bennett subsequently requested a judicial recount, and on the recount, the seat swung back to Bennett with a majority of nine votes.<ref>{{cite press release |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2011/waitakere-judicial-recount-official-results |title=Waitakere Judicial Recount Official Results |publisher=Electoral Commission |date= 17 December 2011 |access-date= 9 November 2023}}</ref> Bennett was declared elected, and Sepuloni was not returned via the party list due to her list ranking, being replaced in the Labour caucus with [[Raymond Huo]].

Five electorates returned with the winner having a majority of less than one thousand – Waitakere (9), Christchurch Central (47), Waimakariri (642), {{NZ electorate link|Auckland Central}} (717) and [[Tāmaki Makaurau (New Zealand electorate)|Tāmaki Makaurau]] (936).<ref name="electorate-status" />

